1.1.text:
Article 7

The obligations and responsibilities of regional government as referred to in Article 6 letter a, include:

a. implementing policies for the protection of women and children from acts of violence in various forms, exploitation, discrimination, maltreatment and/or treatment that degrades human dignity and worth as stipulated by the government;
b. establishing policies, programs, and activities for the protection of women and children from acts of violence in various forms, exploitation, discrimination, maltreatment and/or treatment that degrades human dignity and worth;
c. conducting cooperation in the implementation of protection for women and children from acts of violence in various forms, exploitation, discrimination, maltreatment and/or treatment that degrades human dignity and worth;
d. providing support for facilities and infrastructure for the implementation of protection for women and children from acts of violence in various forms, exploitation, discrimination, maltreatment and/or treatment that degrades human dignity and worth;
e. allocating budget for the implementation of protection for women and children from acts of violence in various forms, exploitation, discrimination, maltreatment and/or treatment that degrades human dignity and worth of women and children in accordance with regional financial capacity; and
f. fostering and supervising the implementation and protection of women and children from acts of violence in various forms, exploitation, discrimination, maltreatment and/or treatment that degrades human dignity and worth.

Article 3

The regulation of the fundamentals of PPA implementation aims to:

a. prevent acts of violence, exploitation, discrimination, mistreatment and/or treatment that degrades the dignity of women and children;
b. eliminate all forms of violence, exploitation, discrimination, mistreatment and/or treatment that degrades the dignity of women and children;
c. protect and provide a sense of security for women and children; d. provide services to women and children victims of violence, reporters, and witnesses; and
e. facilitate and mediate domestic disputes to realize the integrity of a harmonious and prosperous household.

Article 4

Women and children who are victims of violence, exploitation, discrimination, maltreatment and/or treatment that degrades human dignity and worth shall be entitled to the following rights:

a. The right to guaranteed space for community participation in government, including in decision/policy making;
b. The right to population documents and civil registration;
c. The right to Legal Aid;
d. The right to be respected for dignity and worth as a human being; e. The right to health and psychological recovery from the suffering experienced by victims;
f. The right to make one's own decisions;
g. The right to obtain information;
h. The right to confidentiality;
i. The right to compensation;
j. The right to social rehabilitation;
k. The right to complaint handling;
l. The right of victims and their families to obtain facilitation in the judicial process; and
m. The right to assistance.

Article 11

The Regional Government is tasked with carrying out prevention, handling and recovery efforts for victims of violence against Women and Children, by:

a. collecting data and information about Women and Children Victims of acts of violence in various forms, exploitation, discrimination, mistreatment and/or treatment that degrades human dignity and honor;
b. conducting socialization of laws and regulations related to PPA;
c. conducting education on anti-violence values in various forms, exploitation, discrimination, mistreatment and/or treatment that degrades human dignity and honor against women and children;
d. preventing child marriage;
e. providing rehabilitation facilities; and
f. conducting monitoring and evaluation of PPA regarding acts of violence in various forms, exploitation, discrimination, mistreatment and/or treatment that degrades human dignity and honor.

Article 5

Children who are victims of violence, exploitation, discrimination, maltreatment and/or treatment that degrades human dignity and worth, in addition to obtaining the rights as referred to in Article 4, shall also obtain special rights, as follows:

a. The right to respect and full utilization for survival, growth and development;
b. The right to basic services;
c. The right to equal protection;
d. The right to be free from various stigma;
e. The right to employment;
f. The right to decent housing and habitable environment;
g. The right to Women's empowerment; and
h. The right to violence prevention.

Article 10

(1) The obligations and responsibilities of Customary Institutions in organizing protection for women and children as referred to in Article 6 letter e, include:

a. customary institutions have the responsibility to uphold the dignity and human rights of women and children in accordance with highly valued humanitarian values;
b. responsible for preventing all forms of violence, exploitation, discrimination, mistreatment and/or treatment that degrades the dignity and honor of women and children in the customary environment;
c. ensuring gender equality and children's rights are respected, without discrimination based on gender or age;
d. responsible for raising awareness among customary communities regarding the rights of women and children, including awareness of the importance of protecting and respecting their rights;
e. organizing education and training programs for members of customary communities, especially customary leaders, on the rights of women and children and effective protection methods;
f. providing psychosocial and legal support to victims of violence, exploitation, discrimination, mistreatment and/or treatment that degrades the dignity and honor of women and children including facilitating victims' access to protection and legal institutions;
g. playing an active role in the reporting and law enforcement process regarding cases of violence, exploitation, discrimination, mistreatment and/or treatment that degrades the dignity and honor of women and children in customary communities;
h. facilitating the establishment of PPA institutions at the customary level tasked with protecting their rights and providing assistance and support to victims;
i. integrating customary values that support equality and PPA into community education and outreach programs; and j. building partnerships with non-governmental organizations, protection institutions, and educational institutions to strengthen PPA efforts. (2) The obligations and responsibilities of Business Entities in organizing protection for women and children as referred to in Article 6 letter e, include:
a. obligated to comply with all laws, regulations, and rules related to PPA at national and regional levels; b. providing a safe and supportive work environment, free from harassment, discrimination, violence, mistreatment and/or treatment that degrades the dignity and honor of women and children;
c. providing shelter for female workers at the workplace; d. establishing partnerships with regional governments in PPA; e. providing empowerment to women and children as a shared responsibility with regional government;
f. conducting education programs in communities around the company to raise awareness about PPA;
g. creating PPA policies, programs, and activities;
h. supporting social programs that enhance women's economic independence and support children's education; i. supporting the establishment of protection and service centers for victims of violence against women and children; and
j. supporting PPA programs in Pulang Pisau Regency through Corporate Social Responsibility instruments and/or assistance in other forms in accordance with applicable laws and regulations.

Article 16

(1) In order to achieve the objectives of organizing PPA as referred to in Article 3, the Regional Government cooperates with : a. Provincial government;

b. Other Regency/City governments;

c. Indonesian National Police;

d. Prosecutor's Office;

e. Courts; and/or

f. non-governmental institutions.

(2) Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1), includes :

a. exchange of data and information;
b. rehabilitation of victims of violence;
c. repatriation and social reintegration;
d. provision of evidence and witnesses;
e. followed up in accordance with applicable law; and f. Prevention efforts to counter criminal acts against Women and children.
(3) Cooperation as referred to in paragraphs (1) and (2) is set forth in the form of a mutual agreement.

Article 18

(1) Regional Government is obligated to provide guidance and supervision of PPA from all forms of violence, discrimination, special protection and other issues.

(2) Guidance as referred to in paragraph (1), includes: a. guidelines and standards for meeting needs;

b. technical guidance and training;

c. provision of facilities;

d. monitoring; and

e. evaluation.

(3) Guidance as referred to in paragraph (2), is in the context of realizing PPA objectives in accordance with minimum service standards.

Article 6

Obligations and responsibilities in the implementation of protection for women and children are joint responsibilities of:

a. regional government;
b. community;
c. family;
d. parents; and
e. non-governmental institutions including Customary Institutions and Business Entities.

